  That has a consequence worth saying plainly, because it shrinks what you asked for:

  [1mSub-project 2 mostly evaporates.[0m A change-event log plus subscriptions exists to answer "what did [3mother people[0m do to
  things I care about." In a single-user app, you [3mare[0m the other people. Building an event bus, a subscription registry,
  and a fan-out matcher here would be architecture cosplay — real code, real bugs, informing you of edits you made
  thirty seconds ago. I'd rather not write it, and I'd rather tell you now than bury it in a spec.

  The revised decomposition is two pieces:

  1. [1mTasks core[0m — model, [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m persistence, create/edit/complete UI.
  2. [1mReminders[0m — the part of "notifications" that survives, if it's the part you meant.

  Which brings me to the only question left before I can present a design:

  [1mThe fork:[0m in a single-user app, a notification is only worth building if it tells you something you [3mdon't already [0m
  [3mknow[0m. Exactly one category qualifies naturally — time. A task you created last Tuesday becoming due today is genuine
  news. An edit you just made is not.

  - [1mDue-date reminders[0m — tasks get an optional due date; the page surfaces due/overdue on load and while open. Real
    value, small build, no fake infrastructure.
  - [1mReminders + change digest[0m — additionally log edits and show "changed since your last visit." Honest use: you left a
    tab open for a week, or you edit on two devices — but [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m doesn't sync across devices, so the second use
    doesn't work.
  - [1mReminders + OS notifications[0m — Notification API so alerts surface outside the tab. Costs a permission prompt, and
    only fires while the page is open, which is the wrong half of the time.
  - [1mDefer notifications entirely[0m — build Tasks core, live with it, see what you actually miss.

  [1mMy recommendation: due-date reminders.[0m It's the only option where the feature has information you lack. If you want
  the door open, note that due dates make the other three cheap to add later — they're all just different presentations
  of the same date comparison, so deferring costs you nothing.

  I'll hold here for your pick, then present the design for Tasks core with reminders in view. Still nothing written to
  disk.

